{"type":"message","id":"e007d139","parentId":"f5b4ea9c","timestamp":"2026-04-03T08:17:40.548Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can see the issue now. Looking at the HTML response carefully, I see:\n\n```html\n<main class=\"flex-1 overflow-auto pt-14 md:pt-0\"><div class=\"min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center\"><p class=\"text-[var(--text-muted)]\">思考中...</p></div><!--$--><!--/$--></main>\n```\n\nThis means the page IS loading (it's the Dashboard app), but the content shows \"思考中...\" (Thinking...) which suggests the JavaScript is loading but the data hasn't loaded yet. This is a hydration/rendering issue - the server-side rendered HTML shows the loading state, but the client-side JavaScript hasn't hydrated/replaced it with the actual content yet.\n\nHowever, the user is saying they see \"Cannot GET /\" - which is a different error message. \"Cannot GET /\" is typically from an Express/connect server when there's no route handler for the root path. But we know Next.js IS running on the server...\n\nWait - \"Cannot GET /\" could be coming from a DIFFERENT service on the user's machine or network, not from the tunnel. Let me think...\n\nActually wait - the lsof output showed the SSH tunnel IS established:\n```\nssh       42572 blocklee    7u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4e128955      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci (LISTEN)\n```\n\nSo the tunnel is listening on localhost:3001. But when the user accesses http://localhost:3001, it could be hitting something else.\n\nActually wait - the user's SSH tunnel command was:\n```\nssh -L 3001:localhost:3000 ubuntu@vm-0-14-ubuntu\n```\n\nBut when I check on the server, the Next.js app is listening on localhost:3000. The tunnel should work. \n\nThe SSH connection shows the tunnel established on port 3001, but something's off with the forwarding. When I look at the actual HTML response, it contains the Dashboard content with \"思考中...\" - so the tunnel IS working and Next.js IS responding. The problem must be something else.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the HTML is rendering the loading state correctly, which means the issue is likely that the user is hitting a different endpoint or there's a path mismatch. Let me reconsider what they're actually requesting - \"Cannot GET /\" suggests the root path works but something else is failing. Or maybe there's an OpenClaw gateway or reverse proxy intercepting the request before it reaches Next.js, which would explain why the tunnel appears established but the response isn't what they expect. The HTML response shows \"思考中...\" in a paragraph tag, which is actually the loading state. The user might be seeing this as a blank or minimal page initially because the JavaScript hasn't fully hydrated yet, making it appear like \"Cannot GET /\" - a rendering issue rather than a routing problem. Or perhaps they're looking at cached content. Let me try having them make an actual request to see what's really being served.","thinkingSignature":"8c75d7b7b79480f85b3f27b2d17b091b23d1e109a8755b4283cf2bf2011fadc7"},{"type":"text","text":"服务器上 curl 测试返回的是完整的 Dashboard HTML（包含\"思考中...\"加载状态），说明服务本身是正常的。\n\n让我帮你在本地测试一下隧道是否真的通：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_ln463i74hcnb_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -v http://localhost:3000 2>&1 | head -30"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":45133,"output":698,"cacheRead":12321,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":58152,"cost":{"input":0.013539899999999999,"output":0.0008376,"cacheRead":0.0007392599999999999,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.015116759999999998}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775204230897,"responseId":"061eaa870896faec160db2ee465dc112"}}
