ned\\\"},null,\\\"$8\\\",0],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",\\\"script-0\\\",{\\\"src\\\":\\\"/_next/static/chunks/_d7fd5935._.js\\\",\\\"async\\\":true,\\\"nonce\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\"},null,\\\"$9\\\",0],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",\\\"script-1\\\",{\\\"src\\\":\\\"/_next/static/chunks/node_modules_next_a760476a._.js\\\",\\\"async\\\":true,\\\"nonce\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\"},null,\\\"$a\\\",0],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",\\\"script-2\\\",{\\\"src\\\":\\\"/_next/static/chunks/app_layout_tsx_1cf6b850._.js\\\",\\\"async\\\":true,\\\"nonce\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\"},null,\\\"$b\\\",0]],\\\"$c\\\"]},null,\\\"$6\\\",1]},null,\\\"$4\\\",0],{\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$7\\\",\\\"c\\\",{\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",\\\"c-page\\\",{\\\"type\\\":\\\"page\\\",\\\"pagePath\\\":\\\"page.tsx\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L36\\\",null,{\\\"Component\\\":\\\"$37\\\",\\\"serverProvidedParams\\\":{\\\"searchParams\\\":{},\\\"params\\\":{},\\\"promises\\\":null}},null,\\\"$35\\\",1]},null,\\\"$34\\\",1],[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",\\\"script-0\\\",{\\\"src\\\":\\\"/_next/static/chunks/_861a90cd._.js\\\",\\\"async\\\":true,\\\"nonce\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\"},null,\\\"$38\\\",0],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"script\\\",\\\"script-1\\\",{\\\"src\\\":\\\"/\r100 34000    0 34000    0     0   129k      0 --:--:-- --:--:-- --:--:--  130k","cwd":"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1775204672985}}
{"type":"message","id":"011751a6","parentId":"fdd60685","timestamp":"2026-04-03T08:25:18.299Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The server shows proper Next.js response. But the user's tunnel via curl shows `Cannot GET /` error. This is really strange.\n\nWait - the error `Cannot GET /` with HTML format `<pre>Cannot GET /</pre>` looks like it could be from an Express server that has no routes defined, NOT from Next.js. \n\nThe key issue is: there's a difference between what the server sees and what the SSH tunnel shows.\n\nActually wait - looking at the SSH command: `ssh -L 3001:localhost:3000 ubuntu@vm-0-14-ubuntu`\n\nWhen user connects to localhost:3001, SSH tunnels to the server's localhost:3000. But from the server's perspective, when it receives a connection to localhost:3000, it goes to the Next.js app. But the user's curl is showing something else.\n\nWait - maybe I need to check if the server is using IPv6 for localhost vs IPv4? Or there's some docker container networking issue?\n\nLet me think... The server has:\n- 172.18.0.2 (Docker interface)\n- 127.0.0.1 (localhost)\n\nThe Next.js app is listening on localhost:3000 and 172.18.0.2:3000.\n\nBut wait - the \"Cannot GET /\" error with `<pre>` format doesn't look like Next.js. It looks like a simple Express/Connect server with no routes. Maybe there's a Docker port mapping issue?\n\nActually, wait. Looking back at the first message where user ran `lsof -i :3000` on their Mac:\n```\nssh       42572 blocklee    7u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4e128955      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci (LISTEN)\nssh       42572 blocklee    8u  IPv4 0xff0a6d7a8de3d6ad      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci (LISTEN)\n\n\n```\n\nThe SSH tunnel is listening on both IPv6 and IPv4. That's correct.\n\nNow, the \"Cannot GET /\" error the user gets - wait, could it be that the user is running the tunnel to the wrong server or there's an old SSH tunnel still running? Actually, looking at the very first `lsof` output from the user's Mac, I see Google Chrome processes connected to localhost:hbci, not to the SSH tunnel. That's strange - where is the actual connection to port 3001 going?\n\nOh wait, looking more carefully at the lsof output from the first message:\n```\nGoogle    31485 blocklee   50u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4e119155      0t0  TCP localhost:54309->localhost:hbci (CLOSE_WAIT)\nGoogle    31485 blocklee   51u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4f4c7955      0t0  TCP localhost:54310->localhost:hbci (ESTABLISHED)\nssh       42572 blocklee    7u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4e128955      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci (LISTEN)\nssh       42572 blocklee    8u  IPv4 0xff0a6d7a8de3d6ad      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci (LISTEN)\nssh       42572 blocklee   13u  IPv6 0xff0a6d7f4f4d2155      0t0  TCP localhost:hbci->localhost:54310 (ESTABLISHED)\n```\n\nThere's an SSH tunnel already running and listening on localhost:hbci (which is port 3001 in decimal, since hbci is the service name for port 3001/tcp). Chrome has an established connection to it, but that looks like an old connection rather than a fresh one from a new curl request.\n\nThe issue might be that when I curl from the user's machine, it's hitting an SSH tunnel that's not actually forwarding traffic anywhere, or the tunnel exists but isn't connected to a working local port forward. The issue could be that curl on the Mac is somehow resolving localhost differently, or perhaps there's an SSH_CONFIG setting on the server that's overriding the tunnel behavior. Let me suggest having the user try binding to a specific IP address instead of localhost, or check if the server has GatewayPorts enabled. But actually, since curl on the server itself works fine and returns Next.js responses, the problem likely isn't with the Next.js setup itself—it's something about how the tunnel is being established or how the server is configured to handle the forwarded connection.\n\nWait, I just realized something from the first diagnostic check. When I ran `ssh -v -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o ConnectTimeout=5 ubuntu@localhost true`, it said \"Connection refused\" for both IPv6 and IPv4 localhost connections. But when the user runs their SSH command to vm-0-14-ubuntu, it works. The server does have SSH available, just not on localhost—it's accessible via the actual hostname or IP instead.\n\nSo the real issue is: my SSH diagnostic was trying to connect to localhost:22, but SSH isn't bound there. The user's actual tunnel command connects to vm-0-14-ubuntu:22 and then forwards to localhost:3000 on that remote server, which works. That part of the chain is fine.\n\nThe problem must be on the server side—something running on port 3000 that accepts the connection but returns \"Cannot GET /\" instead of the Next.js app. I should restart the Next.js service and have the user try the tunnel again, but first I need to understand why the tunnel worked initially and then started failing. I'm also wondering if the issue is with how the SSH tunnel itself is configured. Try using the internal Docker IP instead:\n```\nssh -L 3001:172.18.0.2:3000 ubuntu@vm-0-14-ubuntu\n```\n\nOr maybe there's an old stale process causing issues. Let me suggest restarting the Next.js service entirely to rule out any zombie processes.","thinkingSignature":"f058935aa566d527a7c2eba4926f9e31dfdce9ecbb0c0cb5ab2d99b2c3ce1ef8"},{"type":"text","text":"这就奇怪了！服务器上 curl localhost:3000 返回的是正确的 Next.js Dashboard HTML，但通过 SSH 隧道 curl localhost:3001 返回的却是 `Cannot GET /`。\n\n这说明可能有**另一个进程**在你的服务器上抢占了 3000 端口。让我重启一下 Dashboard 服务：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_7d5zksfcwega_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"ps aux | grep -E \"next|node\" | grep -v grep"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":77185,"output":1387,"cacheRead":12321,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":90893,"cost":{"input":0.0231555,"output":0.0016644,"cacheRead":0.0007392599999999999,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.025559159999999997}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775204673026,"responseId":"061eac4189914e79075491887d82bef5"}}
